the latest figures from public health  england show another jump in cases of  the  indian variant here in the uk in the  last week there have been another  55 people identified as having the  mutated version of the virus which is  known scientifically as b1617 that  brings the total to 132  but government scientists believe there  are more going undetected  now 94 of those have been passengers  entering the uk or their immediate  contacts  but significantly three cases have no  travel history another 22 are still  being investigated  that shows the old travel rules  requiring self-isolation at home  didn't contain the virus and has begun  to spread within the uk  the first known case was identified on  march the 11th  and they've been rising steadily ever  since there have been eight reported  cases in wales  and five in scotland but the majority  119  have been in england with london the  hotspot accounting for 55 of the cases  the virus is a concern because it's  evolved two significant mutations  that may make it more infectious and  help it evade the immune system  it's still not known how well the  vaccines work against the variant  but separate research does show the  vaccines are highly effective against  the more common strains of the virus  after one dose symptomatic infections  fell by 74  they're the ones that can lead to  serious disease and death silent  infections though which have no  symptoms only fell by 57 that's a worry  because  people can spread the virus without  knowing even after vaccination  but scientists advising the government  say vaccines are working so well  and people are generally still cautious  about mixing widely  that cases should fall to low levels in  may and could even  allow people to go without masks over  the summer before rising later in the  autumn  that assumes the variants remain under  control  if they spread they could put back our  attempts to return to a normal life
 

